WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.531 --> 00:00:02.261
- I was pretty much afraid of everything.

00:00:02.261 --> 00:00:04.142
Afraid of the world, afraid of speaking.

00:00:04.142 --> 00:00:06.302
You know, really, really shy kid.

00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:08.856
So, yeah, we went to a place
that no one spoke English.

00:00:08.856 --> 00:00:11.456
People were telling us, "You know,

00:00:11.456 --> 00:00:13.875
"I learned English from your lyrics."

00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:16.025
Like, oh (muted) man.

00:00:16.025 --> 00:00:18.059
- Hello believe nation, my
name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:20.016
My one word is believe, and I believe

00:00:20.016 --> 00:00:22.331
that entrepreneurs will
solve all of the world's

00:00:22.331 --> 00:00:24.764
major problems, so to
help you on your journey,

00:00:24.764 --> 00:00:26.650
today we're going to
learn from the co-founder

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.977
and main songwriter from
Metallica, James Hetfield,

00:00:29.977 --> 00:00:33.080
and my take on his top
10 rules for success.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.212
Rule number 10 is my personal favorite,

00:00:35.212 --> 00:00:37.891
and I'd love to know which
ones you guys like the best.

00:00:37.891 --> 00:00:40.073
And as always, as you're
watching, if you hear something

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.141
that really resonates
with you, please leave it

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:43.923
in the comments below,
put quotes around it

00:00:43.923 --> 00:00:45.663
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:47.392
You might always win a prize as well.

00:00:47.392 --> 00:00:49.294
And also when you write it
down, it's much more likely

00:00:49.294 --> 00:00:52.377
to stick for yourself as well, enjoy.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.153
(upbeat music)

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:09.030
(hard driving rock music)

00:01:20.566 --> 00:01:22.231
- I was pretty much afraid of everything.

00:01:22.231 --> 00:01:24.409
Afraid of the world, afraid of speaking,

00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:26.735
you know, really, really shy kid.

00:01:26.735 --> 00:01:30.405
Music was a way to
speak, as simple as that.

00:01:30.405 --> 00:01:32.780
I could either just journal, write poetry,

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.026
write just whatever was in my head

00:01:35.026 --> 00:01:38.570
and sit down, strum a few
chords and put that together.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:42.730
And that's me, that's me
telling the world about me

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.738
when I can't do it on my own.

00:01:44.738 --> 00:01:47.849
So music was the voice I didn't have.

00:01:47.849 --> 00:01:50.087
- I wanted to talk to you about,

00:01:50.087 --> 00:01:53.347
there's a transition that
very successful people make,

00:01:53.347 --> 00:01:55.286
and either they make it or they don't.

00:01:55.286 --> 00:01:58.531
And the transition is
to go from being hungry

00:01:58.531 --> 00:02:02.014
and filled with all this angst
to being like stupid wealthy

00:02:02.014 --> 00:02:06.275
and famous but still
keeping your art relevant.

00:02:06.275 --> 00:02:07.858
How do you do that?

00:02:09.215 --> 00:02:11.384
- We're super competitive people.

00:02:11.384 --> 00:02:14.263
We're really perfectionists and we hate

00:02:14.263 --> 00:02:16.824
to let each other down.

00:02:16.824 --> 00:02:19.662
There's always a better riff coming.

00:02:19.662 --> 00:02:22.187
There's always a better album.

00:02:22.187 --> 00:02:24.724
Maybe there's the never
satisfied part to us

00:02:24.724 --> 00:02:27.614
that will keep us going til we die.

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:29.464
There's always the ultimate lyric

00:02:29.464 --> 00:02:32.014
that's going to connect
to everyone in the world,

00:02:32.014 --> 00:02:36.354
or something, there is
always a better something

00:02:36.354 --> 00:02:38.008
that we haven't got yet.

00:02:38.008 --> 00:02:40.793
- How do you stay
grounded, how do you do it?

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:44.243
- Well, I got a (laughs), I got a wife

00:02:44.243 --> 00:02:47.029
that kicks my (bleep) first of all,

00:02:47.029 --> 00:02:49.446
kids that tell it like it is.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:54.336
That to me is love right
there, when your kids,

00:02:54.336 --> 00:02:57.171
"Dad, you're being a jerk."

00:02:57.171 --> 00:03:00.602
I love that, that's what helps me a lot.

00:03:00.602 --> 00:03:03.227
Family certainly does that.

00:03:03.227 --> 00:03:05.197
I think the guys in the band too,

00:03:05.197 --> 00:03:07.864
we all keep each other in check.

00:03:09.477 --> 00:03:13.921
"Dude, is that limo a
little too long, come on."

00:03:13.921 --> 00:03:16.240
Just doofy things like that, you know?

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.907
We try and keep each other real.

00:03:19.811 --> 00:03:23.934
So I think not surrounding
ourselves with yes men, too,

00:03:23.934 --> 00:03:27.373
which can really just
totally distort your reality.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.623
Most things in lie that are worthwhile,

00:03:31.871 --> 00:03:34.177
you got to work at, you got to work at.

00:03:34.177 --> 00:03:36.621
You get out what you put
in, as simple as that.

00:03:36.621 --> 00:03:38.836
That's kind of a universal law.

00:03:38.836 --> 00:03:42.493
So yeah, it's not pick
up the guitar and go,

00:03:42.493 --> 00:03:47.417
"Ha ha," which I've done in
front of the mirror lots.

00:03:47.417 --> 00:03:49.848
"Hey, I'm really good,"
and you got to have

00:03:49.848 --> 00:03:52.254
that attitude too, but it does take work.

00:03:52.254 --> 00:03:55.909
It does take some dedication, you know.

00:03:55.909 --> 00:04:00.540
But if you love doing it, it's
not as hard as it could be

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.591
if you're doing something that
you're not as interested in.

00:04:04.591 --> 00:04:07.417
- How many people have a
dream when they're kids

00:04:07.417 --> 00:04:09.242
of being a (muted) rock star?

00:04:09.242 --> 00:04:11.721
How many people get to be in Metallica?

00:04:11.721 --> 00:04:15.257
- It's crazy dude, I wake
up every day and think,

00:04:15.257 --> 00:04:20.068
"I'm friggin' blessed, I
mean this is unbelievable."

00:04:20.068 --> 00:04:23.771
If I complain once today,
punch me in the face.

00:04:23.771 --> 00:04:26.716
Because it's stupid, this is stupid

00:04:26.716 --> 00:04:29.927
that I would complain about something.

00:04:29.927 --> 00:04:32.844
But I tell ya, it is hard at times,

00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:36.123
being on the road, doing stuff.

00:04:36.123 --> 00:04:38.263
And you don't want to do stuff you do it,

00:04:38.263 --> 00:04:41.243
but then you find
something, you find someone,

00:04:41.243 --> 00:04:45.333
you find a thing, and it's
usually someone in the crowd

00:04:45.333 --> 00:04:48.774
that's just, I see myself right there.

00:04:48.774 --> 00:04:51.204
It's like, I'm that kid right there

00:04:51.204 --> 00:04:53.782
at the Motorhead concert
looking up at Lemmy going,

00:04:53.782 --> 00:04:57.111
"This is (muted) awesome!"

00:04:57.111 --> 00:05:00.937
And so I just suck that energy and go,

00:05:00.937 --> 00:05:03.993
"Alright, this is all I need.

00:05:03.993 --> 00:05:06.153
"This is all I need right now."

00:05:06.153 --> 00:05:09.653
- [Blond Man] What do you
most credit with the success,

00:05:09.653 --> 00:05:13.092
the ongoing, the continued
success of Metallica?

00:05:13.092 --> 00:05:16.306
If there's one thing that
is really the core of that.

00:05:16.306 --> 00:05:18.723
- Honesty, for sure, honesty.

00:05:21.031 --> 00:05:23.940
Whether it's in some kind of monster movie

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.440
or the music or a live show, being honest.

00:05:28.493 --> 00:05:32.795
At the show last night,
the One intro ends,

00:05:32.795 --> 00:05:36.639
intro to One, I go out and I
start playing Fade to Black.

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:38.585
It's like, what the hell?

00:05:38.585 --> 00:05:42.580
And me realizing and getting
that wave of like shame,

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.848
like, "Oh my God, you're an idiot."

00:05:44.848 --> 00:05:46.939
And you're like, "Alright,
just laugh at it,

00:05:46.939 --> 00:05:48.760
"because it's kind of funny."

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.841
And what else are you going to do?

00:05:51.841 --> 00:05:55.881
And then start the One song
and then later talk about it.

00:05:55.881 --> 00:05:58.922
So being honest about it is so freeing,

00:05:58.922 --> 00:06:02.685
being honest with each
other, with ourselves.

00:06:02.685 --> 00:06:05.548
We're 53, what's it look
like, what are we going to do?

00:06:05.548 --> 00:06:07.298
How long can we tour?

00:06:10.441 --> 00:06:13.298
How can we stay healthy and happy?

00:06:13.298 --> 00:06:15.184
So being honest with each other.

00:06:15.184 --> 00:06:17.220
I think being on stage is a different hat.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.014
It's a different mindset,
it's a different space.

00:06:20.014 --> 00:06:23.431
It's, to me, time stops, there's no pain,

00:06:25.812 --> 00:06:29.292
it's like when people, artists talk about

00:06:29.292 --> 00:06:32.124
being in the zone, that is the zone.

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:34.864
I could get in that zone even here,

00:06:34.864 --> 00:06:37.448
just sitting playing and
forget you guys are here.

00:06:37.448 --> 00:06:39.979
Somehow the world goes away.

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:43.149
Whether that's good or bad (laughing).

00:06:43.149 --> 00:06:46.443
It's difficult to come out of sometimes,

00:06:46.443 --> 00:06:49.076
that's the curse of
it, but the blessing is

00:06:49.076 --> 00:06:53.337
there is that focus and there
is that sense of purpose,

00:06:53.337 --> 00:06:56.459
sense of being, sense of communication,

00:06:56.459 --> 00:06:57.959
sense of creation.

00:06:58.793 --> 00:07:00.876
That's a reason I'm here.

00:07:02.244 --> 00:07:05.444
There's times when I haven't
played for a couple weeks

00:07:05.444 --> 00:07:08.942
and my wife will say, "God, you're grumpy.

00:07:08.942 --> 00:07:10.389
"Go play guitar."

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:12.263
It's like, "Oh yeah,"
and I'll go and I'll play

00:07:12.263 --> 00:07:14.344
and then, "Oh, I feel better now."

00:07:14.344 --> 00:07:17.684
It's just what it is, it's a part of me.

00:07:17.684 --> 00:07:19.633
It's like I got to have it.

00:07:19.633 --> 00:07:22.038
It's my coffee in the morning, too.

00:07:22.038 --> 00:07:24.773
And it's my chill in the evening.

00:07:24.773 --> 00:07:27.667
- Do you still get nervous
before you play a show?

00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:30.084
- I'm nervous right now, yes.

00:07:33.855 --> 00:07:35.736
My theory is, if you're not nervous,

00:07:35.736 --> 00:07:38.992
then something's wrong, because
there's some intense stuff

00:07:38.992 --> 00:07:41.075
about to happen and yeah,

00:07:42.189 --> 00:07:45.681
there's always the
jitters before you go out.

00:07:45.681 --> 00:07:48.608
And we have our little huddle and kind of

00:07:48.608 --> 00:07:51.262
calm each other down and say,

00:07:51.262 --> 00:07:55.635
"Hey, whatever happens
is supposed to happen."

00:07:55.635 --> 00:07:57.512
We try not to take
ourselves too seriously,

00:07:57.512 --> 00:08:00.940
but yeah, we're nervous,
because it's a big deal

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.523
and it's important.

00:08:04.032 --> 00:08:07.011
Every little thing that
we've done in our careers,

00:08:07.011 --> 00:08:11.094
anything that's maybe got
in our way or showed up

00:08:12.161 --> 00:08:16.276
in our pathway we thought,
"Wow, this is great,

00:08:16.276 --> 00:08:19.662
"this is the coolest, we've made it."

00:08:19.662 --> 00:08:21.777
We're on tour, we have a
bus, or we have a record

00:08:21.777 --> 00:08:24.420
or we're on someone else's big tour.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.569
There's been so many
great little successes

00:08:27.569 --> 00:08:30.755
along the whole way, and I'd say

00:08:30.755 --> 00:08:34.622
after 36 years still loving
and caring for what we do

00:08:34.622 --> 00:08:37.272
and seeing the great results from it,

00:08:37.272 --> 00:08:41.439
it's more than any dream
that we could have dreamt

00:08:42.371 --> 00:08:44.703
that's come true for us.

00:08:44.703 --> 00:08:47.443
We've played in front of
half a million people.

00:08:47.443 --> 00:08:48.768
- Half a million people?

00:08:48.768 --> 00:08:49.827
- Yeah.
- Where was that?

00:08:49.827 --> 00:08:53.282
- This was in Russia,
this was an airfield.

00:08:53.282 --> 00:08:55.396
- Oh my God.
- So we've done that.

00:08:55.396 --> 00:08:58.606
- [DJ] What the (muted) is that like?

00:08:58.606 --> 00:09:01.174
- It was a sea of Russian people.

00:09:01.174 --> 00:09:02.630
I mean, that's what it was.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:04.800
- [DJ] That's insane to
even see that many people.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.373
- This was in 1991, '91
when the curtain came down

00:09:08.373 --> 00:09:11.623
and all of that, and Time Warner had a,

00:09:14.583 --> 00:09:16.980
they wanted to basically
get their foot in the door

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:19.897
and so they created a free concert.

00:09:22.038 --> 00:09:25.844
It was AC/DC, us, Pantera,

00:09:25.844 --> 00:09:28.158
and I think maybe a local band,

00:09:28.158 --> 00:09:31.726
or some other bands, I'm
sorry if I can't remember.

00:09:31.726 --> 00:09:34.449
They flew us in there, we
landed in this air field,

00:09:34.449 --> 00:09:37.427
oh, Motley Crue was on it.

00:09:37.427 --> 00:09:40.891
Landed in this air field, and
as far as the eye could see,

00:09:40.891 --> 00:09:43.928
I mean it was a free concert,
and it was just people.

00:09:43.928 --> 00:09:47.095
And we get on stage and do what we do.

00:09:49.392 --> 00:09:51.744
It was military, military in the front.

00:09:51.744 --> 00:09:56.297
- [DJ] Jesus Christ, look
at that photo, holy (muted).

00:09:56.297 --> 00:10:00.779
Holy (muted)m Jamie, go
full screen with that.

00:10:00.779 --> 00:10:04.016
Just go with that one,
what in the (muted)?

00:10:04.016 --> 00:10:06.623
Oh my God, that's insane.

00:10:06.623 --> 00:10:08.879
- [James] Is that it, is that the one?

00:10:08.879 --> 00:10:11.070
That might be something else.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:12.449
- [Man] It looks a little
newer but it says here

00:10:12.449 --> 00:10:13.943
it's from '91 Moscow, so.

00:10:13.943 --> 00:10:16.750
- [James] Wow, yeah, pretty remarkable.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:19.412
And there were, imagine a
sea of people like that.

00:10:19.412 --> 00:10:21.104
And see there's a helicopter.

00:10:21.104 --> 00:10:24.427
Helicopters flying over people.

00:10:24.427 --> 00:10:27.296
What if that thing goes down, man?

00:10:27.296 --> 00:10:29.054
You could be chopping
up all kinds of folks.

00:10:29.054 --> 00:10:31.800
- [DJ] That photo, go
right up there, that one,

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.785
on stage facing those people.

00:10:33.785 --> 00:10:37.721
Oh my God, that is (muted) insane.

00:10:37.721 --> 00:10:40.242
- And that helicopter
would come down close

00:10:40.242 --> 00:10:42.563
and they were yelling
at people, like, "Stop!"

00:10:42.563 --> 00:10:44.174
People were moshing or going crazy.

00:10:44.174 --> 00:10:45.852
They didn't know what that was.

00:10:45.852 --> 00:10:48.520
- [DJ] Oh, they thought
it was like a fight.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:49.959
- They're a friggin'
communist country, man,

00:10:49.959 --> 00:10:52.195
and they're like, "People
are getting out of order,

00:10:52.195 --> 00:10:53.310
"this is chaos!"

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:54.526
- [DJ] They didn't have
the internet then either.

00:10:54.526 --> 00:10:57.172
- No way, they didn't know
what to expect of that.

00:10:57.172 --> 00:10:59.881
So there are people
throwing, hitting people

00:10:59.881 --> 00:11:03.566
from the helicopter, and
down in the front there

00:11:03.566 --> 00:11:06.536
there were guys in uniforms.

00:11:06.536 --> 00:11:09.012
Police, military, same thing.

00:11:09.012 --> 00:11:10.715
So they're standing
there in their uniforms

00:11:10.715 --> 00:11:13.234
and after like three or
four songs, they're like,

00:11:13.234 --> 00:11:15.596
"(muted) this" and they
took off their stuff

00:11:15.596 --> 00:11:18.585
and they're out there head
banging and having a good time.

00:11:18.585 --> 00:11:21.168
So we saw the transformation of

00:11:24.106 --> 00:11:27.088
a closed down society to freedom

00:11:27.088 --> 00:11:31.918
right before us, individually
in people, it was awesome.

00:11:31.918 --> 00:11:34.895
- Wow, what does it feel like to stand

00:11:34.895 --> 00:11:37.145
in front of 500,000 people?

00:11:38.791 --> 00:11:40.909
What does this sound like?

00:11:40.909 --> 00:11:43.784
What is the sound of their roars like?

00:11:43.784 --> 00:11:46.117
- Pretty massive, obviously.

00:11:47.205 --> 00:11:49.082
I lost count at like, I don't know,

00:11:49.082 --> 00:11:51.832
three hundred and something thousand.

00:11:51.832 --> 00:11:53.270
And they don't speak your language.

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:57.612
That's just such a, that's
the power of music, man.

00:11:57.612 --> 00:11:59.100
That's a language in its own.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.390
It connects people, it is so cool.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:04.003
So yeah, we went to a place
and no one spoke English.

00:12:04.003 --> 00:12:06.676
Or people were telling us, "You know,

00:12:06.676 --> 00:12:09.006
"I learned English from your lyrics."

00:12:09.006 --> 00:12:11.996
Like, oh (muted) man, I failed English,

00:12:11.996 --> 00:12:15.856
just so you know, I didn't
do so well in school.

00:12:15.856 --> 00:12:17.371
It's pretty bizarre, yeah.

00:12:17.371 --> 00:12:19.025
- [DJ] That's intense.

